    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-221703",
      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "Disprove the following statement:\n\n$7n-2$ is odd for any $n\\in Z.$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}} \n$7n-2$ is {{field-4}}.\n\nNotice that by substituting $n=$ {{field-5}} into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7n-2 & =7({{field-6}})-2 \\\\  & ={{field-7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is {{field-8}}. \n\nSo, $n=$ {{field-9}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.",
      "worked_solution": "Our statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀n\\in Z,\\,7n-2\\text{ is odd}.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n¬(∀n\\in Z,\\,7n-2\\text{ is odd})\\,≡\\,∃n\\in Z,\\,7n-2\\text{ is even}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $n$ such that $7n-2$ is even.\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $n=2$ suffices:\n\nNotice that by substituting $n=2$ into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7n-2 & =7(2)-2 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is even. \n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, $n=2$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
